<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dnieper</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dnieper is a major river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, flowing through present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. It was a central route of trade linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and the Rus' to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. The growth of Rus' power along the Dnieper contributed to the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dnieper.jpg" alt="Dnieper" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dmitry A. Mottl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dniepr_river_in_Kyiv.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
